@page "/components/markdown"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Markdown

<DocsHeader Title="Markdown" Eyebrow="Writing">
    <Lead>Write and show formatted text that is easy to store, compare and search.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    <code>Stil.Markdown</code> is a package of its own, built on Markdig, so the core <code>Stil</code> package stays free
    of dependencies: <code>dotnet add package Stil.Markdown</code> and <code>@@using Stil.Markdown</code>.
    It is safe by default: raw HTML in the Markdown is shown as text, and links and images only go to http, https,
    mailto and tel addresses or relative ones. It supports CommonMark with tables, task lists, autolinks and
    strikethrough.
</p>

<h2 id="notes">Write and show</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Notes)">
    <p>
        <code>StilMarkdownEditor</code> binds like a text area and works with EditForm validation. Its toolbar has bold,
        italic, heading, lists, quote, code and link, and Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I and Ctrl+K work in the text; Ctrl+Z undoes a
        change. Write and Preview switch to a preview. With <code>OnImageUpload</code> there is an image button: the app
        stores the image and returns its URL, which is inserted as Markdown. <code>StilMarkdown</code> shows the text
        with Stil's typography (<code>stil-prose</code>), also in static SSR.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="side-by-side">Preview beside the text</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(SideBySide)">
    <p>
        <code>Preview="StilMarkdownPreview.Side"</code> shows the preview beside the text when the editor is at least 40rem
        wide, and under it when it is narrower. <code>None</code> leaves it out.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="server">On the server</h2>
<p>
    <code>StilMarkdownRenderer.ToHtml(markdown)</code> gives the same safe HTML, for example for an e-mail. Tables get
    the <code>stil-table</code> class.
</p>

